Types of Behavioral Design Patterns

Here are the most commonly used ones:

1. Chain of Responsibility :
============================
Passes a request along a chain of handlers.
Each handler decides whether to process it or pass it.

👉 Example: Approval system (Manager → Director → CEO)

2. Command  :
==================
Encapsulates a request as an object.
Allows parameterization of clients with different requests.

👉 Example: Remote control (buttons mapped to commands)

You can relate this to:

Spring Boot Controllers → Services (loosely similar flow)
Message Queues (Kafka, RabbitMQ) → commands as events
Transaction systems → retry/rollback
Job schedulers → execute commands later

Key Components
--------------------
Command (Interface) → declares execution
Concrete Command → implements action
Receiver → actual business logic
Invoker → triggers command
Client → sets everything

3. Interpreter
==================
Defines grammar for a language and interprets sentences.

👉 Example: SQL parser, expression evaluator

4. Iterator 
==================
Provides a way to access elements of a collection sequentially.

👉 Example: Java Iterator interface

5. Mediator
===================
Reduces direct communication between objects.
Objects communicate via a mediator.

👉 Example: Chat room system

6. Memento
=================
Saves and restores object state without exposing details.

👉 Example: Undo/Redo functionality

7. Observer
===============
One-to-many dependency between objects.
When one object changes, all dependents are notified.

👉 Example: Event listeners, Notification system

8. State
==============
Object behavior changes based on its internal state.

👉 Example: ATM machine states

9. Strategy
===============
Defines a family of algorithms and makes them interchangeable.

👉 Example: Payment methods (Credit Card, UPI, Cash)

10. Template Method
===============
Defines skeleton of an algorithm, subclasses override steps.

👉 Example: Abstract class with common workflow

11. Visitor
=================
Adds new operations to objects without modifying them.

👉 Example: Compiler operations on AST

🔹 Real-Life Example

============================
Think of a food delivery app:

Strategy → different payment options
Observer → order status updates
Chain of Responsibility → order approval system
Command → placing/canceling orders


🔹 Advantages

✔ Loose coupling between objects
✔ Easier communication management
✔ Improves flexibility and scalability
✔ Promotes reusable code